// Fantasy: original stories written for this game. Format: docs/v3/BLUEPRINT-FORMAT.md.

== fantasy.f01 | feature | three-act
title: The Skin In The Rafters
genre: fantasy + fairy-tale
tone: Grey sea, peat smoke and plain speech; wonder treated as weather
era: 1883 | period
setting: A herring station in Orkney | fictional | island
logline: In the last great herring summer, a gutting girl discovers that the sealskin her father hid in the boat-shed roof belongs to her mother, and that giving it back means losing her.
premise: Everyone on the island knows the story: a man finds a seal-woman's skin on the rocks, hides it, and she must stay and be his wife until she finds it. Nobody thinks it happened to anyone they know. {isla}'s mother {mairi} has never once gone in the sea. She stands at the shore every seventh evening, and the seals come in close and look.
synopsis: {isla} is sixteen, fast with a gutting knife and due to follow the fleet south with the other girls. Mending the boat-shed thatch for her father {magnus}, she finds an oilcloth bundle in the rafters that is warm. The first act is a daughter working out what the story means when it is her own house: that her father, a decent, quiet man, did a terrible thing twenty years ago; that her mother's patience is not contentment; that she herself is half of something. She can hold her breath for four minutes and has never thought about it. The second act is the choice the tale never gives to the child. The minister, {reverend_bain}, calls such talk heathen and suspects the gutting girls of everything. The herring are failing; the old men say the sea is owed. {magnus}, confronted, does not deny it. He says he has been a good husband, which is true, and that she would have left, which is also true. {mairi} asks {isla} for nothing. She says only that she has counted the tides. {isla} tries keeping the skin herself, as her own inheritance, and her mother's face tells her what that makes her. In the third act a boat is lost in a squall with {magnus} aboard. {isla} runs to the shed and puts the bundle in her mother's hands, not as a bargain. {mairi} is gone into the surf before she can speak. The men are found on a skerry at dawn, set there by something that took the cork line in its teeth. {mairi} does not come back to the house. On the seventh evening {isla} walks into the sea to her waist and stays under for four minutes, and something grey turns beside her.
question: Is it love if the door was locked?
themes: consent and custom; what children owe to a wrong they were born from; the sea as a creditor
audience: Older children and adults; lovers of folk tales
viewpoint: With {isla}; her mother is seen only from outside until the end
stakes: A marriage, a mother, a father's life, and what {isla} will be if she keeps what is not hers
conflict: A daughter's love for both parents against the fact that one of them is the other's captive
ending: She gives the skin back freely. Her mother goes, saves the boat's crew, and does not return to land. {isla} goes to the water.
experience: A slow cold wonder, a hard moral knot, and a parting that is also a meeting
look: Low stone, silver fish, grey light; the skin always darker and wetter than anything near it
sound: Gulls, knives on barrels, a tune hummed without words. Seals calling at dusk.
m: understand | family | love-vs-duty | bittersweet
@ isla | Isla Flett | lead | protagonist | human | she/her | 16 | To know what she is | She has always been the best swimmer and never wondered why | The truth costs her a parent whichever way she turns | Quick, loyal, slow to judge | From keeping a secret to giving it away | Orkney plainness, few words | Oilskin apron, bandaged gutting fingers
@ mairi | Mairi Flett | co-lead | none | supernatural | she/her | - | The sea | It is where she is from | A skin she has looked for for twenty years | Gentle, watchful, entirely unresigned | From waiting to gone | Speaks rarely and never of herself | Dark hair never quite dry, bare feet in all weathers
@ magnus | Magnus Flett | supporting | antagonist | human | he/him | 47 | To keep his family as it is | He was lonely, and he found a skin | He has been kind for twenty years on top of one theft | Quiet, steady, guilty | From hiding to admitting to being saved by her | Says little; the truth when asked | Gansey, sea boots, hands like rope
@ reverend_bain | Reverend Hector Bain | featured | foil | human | he/him | 55 | A godly island | The herring money has brought girls, drink and stories | He has no hold on the sea | Stern, sincere, a little afraid of the shore | Unchanged; he blesses the rescued and asks no questions | Pulpit Scots | Black coat, hat held on in the wind
~ isla - mairi | daughter and mother | love without questions | a bundle put into her hands
~ isla - magnus | daughter and father | trust | the truth told, and a rescue he does not deserve
~ mairi - magnus | wife and husband, as the island sees it | twenty patient years | she saves him and does not come home
